; 17-local.kurt

; Not every `use`/`def`/proved theorem in a loaded file becomes available to
; you. Whether one does depends entirely on its label (10-load.kurt, 02-use.kurt,
; 12-def.kurt): a labelled fact is *exported*, an unlabelled one -- or one
; explicitly labelled `local` -- stays inside the file that wrote it.

load local-helper

P                      ; fine -- "exported" was labelled, so it travelled here

; `Q` and `P implies Q` were never exported (one `local`, one unlabelled) --
; try uncommenting either line below and you'll get an error, since neither
; symbol nor fact ever arrived here:
;
;   Q
;   use Q

; a symbol itself is exported exactly when some exported fact actually
; mentions it -- there's no separate "export this symbol" step. `P` came
; along because the exported fact "exported" mentions it; `Q` didn't, because
; nothing exported mentions it.

; `local` works the same way for `def` -- a defined symbol and the fact
; defining it travel together, or stay together:
load prop
bool R
use R
def S iff R  local "s-hidden"    ; both `S` and its meaning stay right here

; this would fail if you tried it from a *different* file that only
; `load`s this one -- `S` was never exported, so it wouldn't exist there:
S iff R
